# Chapter 1 of Design of Everyday Things

So I read chapter 1 and I found like most other textbooks, the actual content inside the book is fairly digestable, if only the author didn't go on tangents about
personal stories they've experienced or use large words. But this journal isn't about what I think about the book's writing, it's what I think about the reading itself. 

Chapter 1 had a big emphasis on "human-centered design" meaning designing for humans. I immediately understood what the term was referring to as I've bore witness 
to many human interactions with objects where they just couldn't figure it out. These personal experiences of mine vary from poor signage at events, poor signifiers of where and how to use, and
just plain not knowing how to use an object. Inside the reading, it was kind of like it was split into several sub-chapters. Each of these sub-chapters had its own term assigned to it like discoverability, signifiers, or
conceptual models. I think it was these sub-chapters that made the reading more digestable as I knew or I could infer what I was about to start learning and that I was moving on from a new topic. 

A quick overview. *Discoverability* is the concept that people should be able to figure out what they can do and what is possible with an object. *Affordance* is the possible actions that can be performed
with an object, like sitting in a chair. *Signifiers* are visible indicaters that communicate to the user where or how to perform an action like the handle of a door. *Mapping* is the relationship between an 
object's controls and what is their results, like knowing what happens when you pull the tab down on a toaster. *Feedback* is how the system communicates that the result of using the object has been performed 
correctly. The last one I remember is *conceptual models* which I believe is the understanding from people on how an object is supposed to be use and what will happen when used. 
It was a pretty good read, I couldn't help but remember hearing from somewhere that lower human intelligence is comparable to smarter bear intelligence while I was reading it
